  <h1>India’s Urban Heat Stress — Climate Change and Public Health</h1>
  <div><em>Indian Express | Date: 2024 • ~600 words</em></div>
  <h3>Introduction</h3><p>Heatwaves are becoming one of the deadliest climate-linked disasters in India. In 2024, Indian Express reported record-breaking heat events across Delhi, Ahmedabad, and Nagpur, exposing the vulnerabilities of urban poor, informal workers, and public health systems. Managing urban heat stress is now a core component of India’s climate adaptation strategy.
________________________________________</p>
  <h3>Body</h3><div>________________________________________<br/>Introduction<br/><br/>Current Status<br/>•	Heatwave Days: IMD recorded 280+ heatwave days in 2023, 20% higher than the 10-year average.<br/>•	Cities Worst Hit: Delhi, Ahmedabad, Nagpur, Lucknow.<br/>•	Mortality: >3,000 deaths attributed to heat stress in 2023–24.<br/>•	Economic Losses: Productivity decline in outdoor labour ~5–10%.<br/>________________________________________<br/>Causes<br/>1.	Climate Change: IPCC projects 2–3°C rise in South Asia by mid-century.<br/>2.	Urban Heat Island Effect: Dense concrete, loss of green cover intensify heat.<br/>3.	Poor Urban Planning: Lack of ventilation, reflective surfaces, and cooling systems.<br/>4.	Vulnerable Populations: Slum dwellers, daily wage workers, elderly, children.<br/>________________________________________<br/>Government Responses<br/>•	National Action Plan on Climate Change (NAPCC): Includes adaptation missions.<br/>•	National Disaster Management Authority (NDMA): Heatwave guidelines issued.<br/>•	State Action Plans: Gujarat pioneered Heat Action Plan (2013); now replicated.<br/>•	Urban Missions: AMRUT, Smart Cities incorporating green cover, water bodies.<br/>________________________________________<br/>Challenges<br/>•	Implementation Gaps: Heat Action Plans often remain on paper.<br/>•	Infrastructure Deficit: Few cooling shelters, poor water access in slums.<br/>•	Public Health Preparedness: Hospitals lack capacity to handle heatstroke surges.<br/>•	Equity Gap: Rich access ACs, poor face life-threatening exposure.<br/>________________________________________<br/>Significance<br/>•	Public Health: Heat stress exacerbates cardiovascular and respiratory illnesses.<br/>•	Economy: Labour productivity decline affects construction, agriculture.<br/>•	Climate Justice: Poor contribute least to emissions but suffer most.<br/>•	Urban Planning: Heat resilience integral to sustainable cities.<br/>________________________________________<br/>UPSC Relevance<br/>•	GS-I (Geography): Climate and disasters.<br/>•	GS-II (Governance): Urban policies, health systems.<br/>•	GS-III (Environment): Adaptation, resilience.<br/>•	Essay:<br/>o	“Heatwaves are silent disasters of climate change.”<br/>o	“Building climate-resilient cities is India’s urgent imperative.”<br/>•	PSIR Paper II: Governance and climate adaptation.<br/>________________________________________<br/>Way Forward<br/>1.	Expand Heat Action Plans: Cover all vulnerable districts with early warning.<br/>2.	Cool Roofs & Green Spaces: Mandate reflective roofs, urban greening.<br/>3.	Public Awareness: Campaigns on hydration, precautions, and first aid.<br/>4.	Workplace Reforms: Adjust working hours for outdoor labourers.<br/>5.	Technology Use: Heat-mapping, satellite data for risk hotspots.<br/>________________________________________<br/>Conclusion</div>
  <h3>Conclusion</h3><p>Urban heat stress is a growing climate and health emergency. Proactive adaptation measures, equitable planning, and robust healthcare systems are critical. For UPSC aspirants, it illustrates the convergence of climate change, urbanisation, and public health in policy debates.</p>
